{
  "index": 22,
  "response": "\nThe sky appears blue because of a phenomenon called Rayleigh scattering, which occurs when sunlight enters Earth's atmosphere. The sunlight encounters tiny molecules of gases in the atmosphere, such as nitrogen and oxygen, that are present in abundance. These molecules scatter the light in all directions, but they scatter shorter (blue) wavelengths more than longer (red) wavelengths.\n\nAs a result of this scattering, the blue light is dispersed throughout the atmosphere, giving the sky its characteristic blue color. The red light, on the other hand, travels through the atmosphere with little scattering and reaches our eyes directly, giving the appearance of a red sunset.\n\nThe exact shade of blue can vary depending on several factors, including the amount of dust and water vapor in the atmosphere, as well as the angle of observation. For example, during sunrise or sunset, when the sun is low on the horizon, the sky may appear more red than blue due to the greater amount of scattering that occurs close to the horizon.\n\nSo, to summarize, the sky appears blue because of the way light interacts with the gases in Earth's atmosphere, particularly nitrogen and oxygen, through a process called Rayleigh scattering.",
